FOR STATIC PAGE

1. Prerequisites
AWS Account
AWS CLI installed (aws configure to set up access keys)
Elastic Beanstalk CLI installed (pip install awsebcli)
Your project folder ready (e.g., index.html, style.css, script.js)

2. Prepare Your Project
Your folder structure should look like this:
my-static-site/
 ├── index.html
 ├── style.css
 ├── script.js

Add a file named application.py (Beanstalk expects an entry point).
But since this is a static site, we’ll use a simple trick:

my-static-site/
 ├── .ebextensions/
 │    └── static-site.config
 ├── index.html
 ├── style.css
 ├── script.js

static-site.config

option_settings:
  aws:elasticbeanstalk:environment:ProxyServer: nginx

This tells Beanstalk to serve static files through Nginx.

3. Initialize Elastic Beanstalk
In your terminal:
cd my-static-site
eb init

Select your region
Choose “Web Server Environment”
Platform: Python or Node.js (either is fine for static hosting)

Enter your app name

4. Create Environment
eb create my-static-env

This will:
Create an EC2 instance (Ubuntu-based)
Configure load balancer and environment

5. Deploy the Site
eb deploy

Wait for deployment to finish.

6. Access Your Website
After deployment:
eb open

This opens your live website URL in the browser.

7. Update Later
When you make changes:
eb deploy

You don’t need to modify any internal Beanstalk config files except adding .ebextensions/static-site.config.
AWS Beanstalk handles instance creation, Nginx setup, and serving your static files automatically.
